          over a websocket to kolu-server (the web shell), which binds padi (the
          per-host workspace daemon), which owns kaval (the PTY daemon) over a
          unix socket. The client and kolu-server are faces that come and go;
          padi and kaval are daemons that survive restarts. kaval holds the live
          PTYs — zsh, claude, nvim.</title
        >
        <defs>
          <marker
            id="ax-arrow"
            viewBox="0 0 10 10"
            refX="8.5"
            refY="5"
            markerWidth="6.5"
            markerHeight="6.5"
            orient="auto-start-reverse"
          >
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="ax-d-arrow"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        {/* grouping brackets */}
        <path d="M160,40 H150 V178 H160" class="ax-d-bracket"></path>
        <text x="86" y="102" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-cap">FACES</text>
        <text x="86" y="119" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub2"
          >come &amp; go</text
        >
        <path d="M160,236 H150 V374 H160" class="ax-d-bracket"></path>
        <text x="86" y="298" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-cap">DAEMONS</text>
        <text x="86" y="315" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub2"
