          >PTY daemon — keeps shells alive</text
        >

        {/* kaval owns the PTYs */}
        <text x="628" y="312" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-cap"
          >OWNS THE PTYS</text
        >
        <path d="M530,354 H556" class="ax-d-flow"></path>
        <path d="M548,354 V332 H564" class="ax-d-flow" marker-end="url(#ax-arrow)"
        ></path>
        <path d="M548,354 H564" class="ax-d-flow" marker-end="url(#ax-arrow)"
        ></path>
        <path
          d="M548,354 V376 H564"
          class="ax-d-flow"
          marker-end="url(#ax-arrow)"></path>
        <rect x="564" y="323" width="132" height="20" rx="6" class="ax-d-pty"
        ></rect>
        <text x="576" y="337" class="ax-d-pty-text">▸ zsh</text>
        <rect x="564" y="345" width="132" height="20" rx="6" class="ax-d-pty"
        ></rect>
        <text x="576" y="359" class="ax-d-pty-text">▸ claude</text>
        <rect x="564" y="367" width="132" height="20" rx="6" class="ax-d-pty"
        ></rect>
        <text x="576" y="381" class="ax-d-pty-text">▸ nvim</text>
      </svg>
    </figure>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="ax-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>layer</th>
            <th>its one job</th>
            <th>killing it does <em>not</em> lose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            layers.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <code class="mono text-ink">{l.name}</code>
                </td>
                <td>{l.job}</td>
                <td>{l.keeps}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── survival / adoption ───────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14">
    <h2 class="ax-head">a deploy <span class="ax-prog">adopts</span> what's running</h2>
    <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Upgrading kolu doesn't kill your daemons. A new build <em>adopts</em> the
      compatible ones — and their live PTYs — rather than reaping and restarting
      them. Restore is the path for a daemon that's actually <em>dead</em>, never
      for one that's still running. So the agents in your terminals survive the
      upgrade: they never stopped.
    </p>

    <figure class="rounded-xl border border-rule px-5 py-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <svg
        class="ax-diagram"
        viewBox="0 0 720 286"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="ax-survive-title"
      >
        <title id="ax-survive-title"
          >A deploy timeline. In the top lane, the running padi vX drains — saves
          its session and exits — and the new build padi vY starts and adopts the
          same kaval. In the bottom lane, kaval runs unbroken straight through the
          deploy, holding every live PTY, so shells and agents never stop.</title
        >
        <defs>
          <marker
            id="ax-arrow2"
            viewBox="0 0 10 10"
            refX="8.5"
            refY="5"
            markerWidth="6.5"
            markerHeight="6.5"
            orient="auto-start-reverse"
          >
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" class="ax-d-arrow"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        {/* deploy marker — touches the top (padi) lane only */}
        <path d="M370,34 V156" class="ax-d-socket"></path>
        <text x="370" y="26" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-cap">DEPLOY</text>

        {/* top lane: padi drains and respawns */}
        <rect x="48" y="64" width="150" height="50" rx="9" class="ax-d-box"
        ></rect>
        <image
          href="/padi-logo.svg"
          x="60"
          y="75"
          width="26"
          height="26"
          preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
          class="ax-d-logo"
        ></image>
        <text x="136" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-label2"
          >padi · vX</text
        >
        <text x="136" y="103" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub"
          >running</text
        >

        <path d="M198,89 H522" class="ax-d-flow" marker-end="url(#ax-arrow2)"
        ></path>
        <text x="360" y="80" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-tlabel"
          >drain → respawn</text
        >
        <text x="360" y="107" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub2"
          >save session · exit · PTYs untouched</text
        >

        <rect x="522" y="64" width="150" height="50" rx="9" class="ax-d-daemon"
        ></rect>
        <image
          href="/padi-logo.svg"
          x="534"
          y="75"
          width="26"
          height="26"
          preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
          class="ax-d-logo"
        ></image>
        <text x="610" y="86" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-daemon-label2"
          >padi · vY</text
        >
        <text x="610" y="103" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub"
          >the new build</text
        >

        {/* vY adopts the surviving kaval */}
        <path d="M597,114 V176" class="ax-d-flow" marker-end="url(#ax-arrow2)"
        ></path>
        <text x="609" y="150" class="ax-d-tlabel">adopts</text>

        {/* bottom lane: kaval, unbroken */}
        <rect x="48" y="176" width="624" height="58" rx="10" class="ax-d-daemon"
        ></rect>
        <image
          href="/kaval-logo.svg"
          x="70"
          y="187"
          width="34"
          height="34"
          preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet"
          class="ax-d-logo"
        ></image>
        <text x="116" y="200" class="ax-d-daemon-label2">kaval</text>
        <text x="116" y="217" class="ax-d-sub">one process · never restarts here</text>

        {/* the PTY stream — unbroken straight through the deploy */}
        <path d="M232,205 H648" class="ax-d-stream"></path>
        <text x="240" y="209" class="ax-d-tick">▸</text>
        <text x="330" y="209" class="ax-d-tick">▸</text>
        <text x="420" y="209" class="ax-d-tick">▸</text>
        <text x="510" y="209" class="ax-d-tick">▸</text>
        <text x="600" y="209" class="ax-d-tick">▸</text>

        <text x="360" y="266" text-anchor="middle" class="ax-d-sub2"
          >your shells + agents stream straight through — scrollback intact</text
        >
      </svg>
    </figure>

    <div class="ax-callout mt-6 max-w-xl">
      <p>
        <strong>Newest wins, and nothing is force-killed.</strong> A newer client
        that meets an older padi asks it to <em>drain</em> — save its session and
        exit, while the PTYs stay alive in kaval — then starts the new build in
        its place. An older client meeting a newer padi is refused, loudly. The
        version handshake runs over a tiny side channel that never changes, so
        upgrades converge on their own. A redeploy that changes padi's
        <em>build</em> but not its version converges the same way — the server
        drains the old build once at boot and brings up its own — so a code
        change reaches you at deploy cadence, not only a version bump.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── vs tmux / zellij ──────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14">
    <h2 class="ax-head">compared to tmux and zellij</h2>
    <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      tmux and zellij fold the PTYs, the layout, and the session into <em>one</em>
      server process. kolu splits them — kaval owns the PTYs, padi owns the
      workspace — so the layer you restart or redeploy isn't the layer holding
      your shells.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="ax-table ax-cmp">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>tmux</th>
            <th>zellij</th>
            <th>kolu</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Detach / close the client</td>
            <td class="c-yes">survives</td>
            <td class="c-yes">survives</td>
            <td class="c-yes">survives — the client holds no state</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Redeploy / restart the session-UI layer</td>
            <td class="c-na">n/a — no separate layer</td>
            <td class="c-na">n/a — no separate layer</td>
            <td class="c-yes">PTYs keep running; the deploy adopts them</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Upgrade the binary under live sessions</td>
            <td class="c-mid">attach breaks on a protocol-version bump</td>
            <td class="c-mid">sessions orphaned on a contract-version change</td>
            <td class="c-yes">compatible daemon + live PTYs adopted; a skewed one is drained, not killed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Machine reboot</td>
            <td class="c-mid">nothing native (a plugin re-runs commands)</td>
            <td class="c-mid">re-runs commands (built-in)</td>
            <td class="c-mid">re-spawns from padi's saved session</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Knows git · PR/CI · agent state</td>
            <td class="c-no">no — status-bar scripting only</td>
            <td class="c-no">no — plugin scripting only</td>
            <td class="c-yes">yes — padi derives it</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Persisted to disk</td>
            <td class="c-no">none native; plugins add layout + cwd</td>
            <td class="c-mid">layout + cwd + each pane's command</td>
            <td class="c-yes">records · session · restore target · agent memory</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Reach</td>
            <td class="c-mid">terminal TUI · single host</td>
            <td class="c-mid">terminal TUI · single host</td>
            <td class="c-yes">browser PWA + kaval-tui; kaval-tui reaches remote hosts over ssh today</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="ax-callout mt-6 max-w-xl">
      <p>
        <strong>The honest caveat: a reboot is the equalizer.</strong> No
        multiplexer keeps a <em>live</em> process across a machine reboot — a PTY
        can't outlive the kernel, so all three re-create rather than resume. kolu
        just remembers more about <em>how</em>: the layout, the working directory,
        the agent that was running, and where to pick it up. Where kolu actually
        differs is the everyday case — restarting or redeploying the workspace
        layer, which for tmux and zellij is the same process that holds your
        shells, and for kolu is not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── the surface framework ─────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14">
    <h2 class="ax-head">one contract — <span class="ax-prog">@kolu/surface</span></h2>
    <p class="ax-readmore mb-5">
      <a href="/blog/surface-framework">
        read the story — <span class="text-ink">Announcing @kolu/surface</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </p>
    <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-6">
      Every layer talks over the same thing: a <em>typed reactive surface</em>.
      You declare what a daemon exposes — a handful of primitives plus the verbs
      to change them — and get a client that stays in sync. Four primitives cover
      almost everything:
    </p>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-8 gap-y-6 max-w-xl">
      {
        primitives.map((p) => (
          <div>
            <code class="mono text-kolu-primary text-sm">{p.sig}</code>
            <p class="mono text-ink-dim text-xs mt-1">{p.q}</p>
            <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed mt-1.5">
              {p.what}
            </p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>

    <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mt-7">
      Alongside them sit <em>procedures</em> — the imperative verbs (create a
      terminal, kill one, restore a session) you call and await. That's the whole
      contract, and it's served over <span class="text-ink">any transport</span>:
      in-process with no wire at all, over a local unix socket, over a browser
      websocket, or over ssh. That ssh transport is <span class="text-ink">already
      live</span> — it's how <code class="mono text-ink">kaval-tui --host</code> and
      drishti reach a daemon on another machine; what's still coming is kolu itself
      using it to bind a <code class="mono text-ink">padi</code> on a remote host.
      Swap the transport; the client code doesn't change. That's exactly how{" "}
      <code class="mono text-ink">padi</code> grew up: it ran in-process inside
      kolu-server, then moved out into its own process behind a unix socket, and
      the client never noticed.
    </p>

    <div class="ax-callout mt-6 max-w-xl">
      <p>
        <strong>The framework stands alone.</strong> It isn't kolu-shaped — a
        separate app,{" "}
        <a
          href="https://github.com/srid/drishti"
          class="text-kolu-primary hover:underline"
          rel="noopener">drishti</a
        >, is built on the same <code class="mono text-ink">@kolu/surface</code>,
        and every change to the framework must ship with drishti's own tests
        green against it. If the contract only fit kolu, drishti couldn't hold.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ── identity ──────────────────────────────────────────── */}
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-14">
    <h2 class="ax-head">identity is a <span class="ax-prog">folder</span>, not a port</h2>
    <p class="text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl mb-5">
      A daemon <em>is</em> its data folder. padi's identity is its <em>state-root</em>
      — a durable directory on the host that holds its session, its memory, its
      activity feed. The socket it listens on is named by a digest of that folder
      and lives in a scratch dir that's wiped on every boot. The port is config;
      the folder is the daemon.
    </p>

    <div class="ax-term max-w-xl">
      <span class="note">// identity — durable, IS the daemon</span>
      <span class="cmd">$XDG_STATE_HOME/padi</span>
      <span class="note">// address — config, wiped on boot</span>
      <span class="cmd">$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/padi-&lt;digest&gt;/sock</span>
      <span class="note"> where &lt;digest&gt; = hash(state-root path)</span>
    </div>

    <ul class="mt-6 text-ink-dim text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed max-w-xl space-y-3">
      <li>
        A stale lock can never make a <span class="text-ink">dead daemon look
        alive</span> — its socket lives in the boot-wiped dir, gone the moment the
        machine restarts.
      </li>
      <li>
        A dev or test kolu gets <span class="text-ink">its own state-root</span> —
        so its own padi, its own kaval. It cannot touch your production
        terminals, ever.
      </li>
      <li>
